# HuntSpill-3-idle-pump.patch (lane HuntSpill, 2026-10-30; the synced tree with every patch in sync/ORDER applied).
# When the last stream finished, rank 0's MultiDecoder.finish and every follower's IDLE op staged every waiting block
# of the spill tier's kept writes through the write ring in one call (BlockStore.stage_all), which waits for the writer
# to free ring slots, up to 10 s; GlmScheduler._iteration sends the finished streams' replies AFTER finish, so the last
# reply's end (an agent's tool call) and the next request's admission waited for the writer: ~(blocks still waiting -
# 21 ring slots) x ~19 ms (the writer's measured rate at TP=3: 67 blocks in 1.26-1.32 s idle, pr124-readfix-tp3), e.g.
# ~5 s after a cold 772k prompt with a short reply. Now an idle rank pumps between short waits for its next request
# (IDLE_POLL_S, 5 ms): rank 0 in GlmScheduler._idle_get (after the replies went out), a follower in its doorbell wait
# (GlmEngine._await_bell(work)); a request arriving is admitted at once and the rounds pump as before. stage_all goes.
# The same blocks, bytes and files; no reply bit is touched (pumping reads rows that are final).
# Measured on the CPU rig (2-slot ring, writer slowed to 25 ms a block, a 30-block state waiting): finish 1,017 ms ->
# 0 ms; a request arriving while blocks wait is taken 0.3 ms after it arrives; the doorbell wait returns at the bell.
# Test: test_idle_pump.py <tree> [--ref <pre-patch tree>] (CPU torch, Linux): the checks above, plus every file on
# disk byte-identical to the pre-patch tree's (34 files); the pre-patch tree FAILS (finish waits ~1 s; no idle pump).
